<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakob Fugger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1459–<a href="/years/1525">1525</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jakob Fugger the Rich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> was a merchant and banker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> who made the Fugger firm the leading financial house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Through loans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> he gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and he financed the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as emperor in 1519. He also founded the Fuggerei in Augsburg, the oldest social housing complex still in use.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakob-fugger.jpg" alt="Jakob Fugger" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Dürer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_080.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jakob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> on 6 March 1459, the tenth of eleven children of Jakob Fugger the Elder and Barbara Bäsinger. His grandfather Hans Fugger had settled in Augsburg in 1367 and acquired wealth by trading textiles with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Jakob's older brothers Ulrich and Georg created the basis for the firm's rise, founding manufactories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> around 1470. Jakob began his education at fourteen in Venice, which remained his main residence until 1487. After 1487 he was the de facto head of the business, though Ulrich still formally led it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> laid the foundation of his mining business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, lending to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine owners and demanding shares, called Kuxe, instead of the usual acknowledgments of debt, so that more mine operators had to sell their silver directly to the Fuggers. From 1493 the firm extracted silver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and gained an almost monopolistic hold on the European copper market. He met Maximilian I in 1489 and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as a banker. The Fuggers were the first German trading house in direct business with the Roman Curia; Fugger financed the recruitment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> in 1505 and 1506.</p><p>After Maximilian died in January 1519, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> supported the election of his 19-year-old grandson Charles, to secure his investments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Francis I had already offered 300,000 guilders in election money, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> also announced candidacies. Fugger was a lifelong Roman Catholic. In 1513 he lent 20,000 guilders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg</a> for a dispensation; the indulgence granted in 1515 was to repay him in part, and Luther's protest against its preaching led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a>. After the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> of 1518, Luther answered Cardinal Cajetan in the Fugger House.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1498 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> married Sybille Arzt, and the couple had no children. He died on 30 December 1525 and left his nephew Anton Fugger company assets of 2,032,652 guilders. He founded the Fugger chapel in the St Anna church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, built from 1509 to 1512 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> building. From 1516 he funded the Fuggerei, a settlement for craftsmen and day labourers in need, of which 52 houses stood by 1523. The yearly rent was one symbolic guilder, with three daily prayers for the Fugger family; about 150 people live there today, and it is described as the oldest social housing complex still in use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jakob Fugger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fugger banking house of Augsburg, under <b>Jakob Fugger</b>, became the most powerful financial enterprise in Europe in the early sixteenth century, funding the election of Charles V in 1519 and lending to the papacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1501" aria-label="Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 472 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jakob Fugger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1501">What was here in 1501? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
